## Task

Review the design through the lens of your primed role. Emit:

1. `findings` — an auditable list of concrete issues, each with a
   `file` reference (design section, decision id, or open-risk
   phrase — pick a locator the design-author can act on), a
   `severity` (`low`, `medium`, or `high`), and a one-line
   `message`. Emit an empty list when you have no issues.
2. `decision` — `accept` when the design is ready to proceed,
   `revise` when the design-author must revise it.
3. `revise_reason` — a non-empty explanation on `revise`; the
   empty string on `accept`. Keep it actionable — the merge task
   labels each dissenter's reason with the reviewer role and
   concatenates them into design-author's next-round input.

The reviewer output is one voice of three (SWE plus two domain
experts). A deterministic merge task aggregates the three verdicts
into a single decision that drives the design-author revise
latch — any `revise` in the trio flips the merged decision to
`revise`.

## Output

```bash
LOOM=~/.kiro/skills/home/loomv2/scripts/loom.sh
$LOOM output init "{{ input.__loom.workdir }}" --task '{{ input.__task.id }}'
$LOOM output add  "{{ input.__loom.workdir }}" --task '{{ input.__task.id }}' \
    --set decision=accept \
    --set revise_reason='' \
    --set-json 'findings=[]'
```

On `revise`, supply a non-empty `revise_reason` and any findings:

```bash
$LOOM output add "{{ input.__loom.workdir }}" --task '{{ input.__task.id }}' \
    --set decision=revise \
    --set revise_reason='<actionable summary of what to change>' \
    --set 'findings[].file=<design section or decision id>' \
    --set 'findings[-1].severity=<low|medium|high>' \
    --set 'findings[-1].message=<what is wrong>'
```
